## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical I/O scheduling knowledge, correctness, and minimal code for a common OS component.

## Key Concepts
- SCAN elevator algorithm
- Head movement and direction reversal
- Service order derivation from a head position
- Edge cases: empty queue, all requests on one side

## Code Example
```c
// toy SCAN scheduler (upward)
int scan_schedule(int head, int req[], int n, int out[]) {
  int up[n], dn[n], upn=0, dnn=0, k=0;
  for (int i=0;i<n;i++) if (req[i] >= head) up[upn++] = req[i]; else dn[dnn++] = req[i];
  qsort(up, upn, sizeof(int), cmp_asc);
  qsort(dn, dnn, sizeof(int), cmp_desc);
  for (int i=0;i<upn;i++) out[k++] = up[i];
  for (int i=0;i<dnn;i++) out[k++] = dn[i];
  return k; // number of entries
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt SCAN for multiple disks or priority requests?
- How would you test correctness with random traces and measure average seek distance?

